---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { MODELS, PLUGIN } from '../config';
import { breadcrumbNode } from '../lib/schema';
---

<Base
  title="Compatibility"
  description={`What AI Blog Writer with Sources supports: WordPress ${PLUGIN.requiresWp}+, PHP ${PLUGIN.requiresPhp}+, OpenAI, Anthropic, Google and xAI models, Rank Math, Yoast SEO, All in One SEO, SEOPress, multisite, and six interface languages.`}
  schema={[breadcrumbNode([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Compatibility', path: '/compatibility/' }])]}
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Compatibility</p>
      <h1>What it works with</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Requirements and supported providers, models and plugins for version {PLUGIN.version} of the free plugin and
        for AI Blog Writer Pro.
      </p>

      <h2>WordPress and PHP</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">WordPress</th><td>{PLUGIN.requiresWp} or later, tested up to {PLUGIN.testedUpTo}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">PHP</th><td>{PLUGIN.requiresPhp} or later</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Editor</th><td>Drafts are core blocks. The sidebar with provenance, sources, section rewrites and SEO suggestions needs the block editor.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Multisite</th><td>Yes. Activate per site or network-wide; each site keeps its own keys, settings, jobs and writer profiles.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Themes</th><td>Any theme that displays core blocks.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Server</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Outbound HTTPS</th><td>To your AI provider's API, and to the source URLs you supply.</td></tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Sodium</th>
              <td>To save API keys encrypted in the database (included in PHP since 7.2). Without it, define the key in <code>wp-config.php</code> instead.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Background jobs</th>
              <td>WP-Cron, or a server scheduler calling <code>wp-cron.php</code> every minute if <code>DISABLE_WP_CRON</code> is set. See <a href="/docs/background-jobs-and-wp-cron/">Background jobs and WP-Cron</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">WP-CLI</th><td>Optional: <code>wp aibcg job run &lt;job-id&gt;</code> runs one stage of a job.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>AI providers and models</h2>
      <p>
        Use your own API key from any of these providers; the model is chosen in Settings. Instead of saving a key, you
        can define it in <code>wp-config.php</code> with the constant shown.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Provider</th><th scope="col">Models</th><th scope="col">Key constant</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {MODELS.map((entry) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{entry.provider}</th>
                <td>{entry.models.map((model, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}{model === entry.recommended ? <strong>{model} (recommended)</strong> : model}</>)}</td>
                <td><code>{entry.constant}</code></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Whether a model is available also depends on your provider account. See <a href="/docs/ai-providers-and-api-keys/">AI providers and API keys</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Plugins and services</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Integration</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Plugin</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Rank Math SEO</th><td>Apply the suggested SEO title, description and focus keyword from the editor sidebar</td><td>Free</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Yoast SEO</th><td>The same, for Yoast SEO</td><td>Pro</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">All in One SEO</th><td>The same, for All in One SEO</td><td>Pro</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">SEOPress</th><td>The same, for SEOPress</td><td>Pro</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Tavily</th><td>Find sources automatically, with your own Tavily key</td><td>Pro</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Languages</h2>
      <p>
        The plugin's screens are available in English, German, French, Spanish, Brazilian Portuguese and Japanese, and
        follow each user's WordPress language. Articles are written in the language set in each brief, in any language
        your AI model writes.
      </p>

      <h2>AI Blog Writer Pro</h2>
      <p>
        AI Blog Writer Pro 1.3.0 needs the free plugin 1.5.0 or later, with the same WordPress and PHP requirements. A
        Creator license covers 1 site and an Agency license 5. See <a href="/docs/ai-blog-writer-pro/">the Pro guide</a> and <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Something missing from this list? Ask on the <a href="https://wordpress.org/support/plugin/luis-rita-ai-blog-writer/">support forum</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  tbody th { white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
